RFC-002
JGPU Execution & Device Abstraction

Status: Draft

Author: Mike McCollum

Requires: RFC-001

Version: 0.1

Abstract

This RFC defines the core execution architecture of JGPU.

JGPU is not a graphics API.

It is a compute execution platform that exposes a single programming model while allowing execution across multiple hardware and software implementations.

Programs written against JGPU must never depend on CUDA, Vulkan, Metal, DirectX, OpenCL, or any vendor-specific runtime.

Instead they target the JGPU Execution Model.

Hardware backends are implementation details.

Motivation

Current GPU programming is fragmented.

Different vendors expose different APIs.

Different operating systems expose different drivers.

Applications become tightly coupled to implementation details rather than computation itself.

JGPU exists to invert that relationship.

Applications target computation.

JGPU selects execution.

Design Goals

The execution model must satisfy the following properties.

Portable
Deterministic
Backend agnostic
Runtime selectable
Thread safe
Serializable
Inspectable
Recoverable

The API should look identical regardless of execution backend.

Core Architecture

The execution pipeline consists of five layers.

Application

↓

JGPU API

↓

Command Encoder

↓

Execution Scheduler

↓

Backend Driver

↓

Hardware

Only the bottom layer is backend-specific.

Everything above remains identical.

Device Model

A Device represents an execution target.

Device

A Device may represent

CUDA GPU
Vulkan Compute Queue
Metal Device
DirectX Compute
CPU Simulator
Software Raster
Remote Cluster

The application cannot distinguish between these through API behavior.

Capabilities are queried explicitly.

Queue Model

Each Device owns one or more execution queues.

Device

 ├── Compute Queue

 ├── Transfer Queue

 └── Async Queue

Queues execute submitted command buffers.

Queue ordering is guaranteed.

Synchronization is explicit.

Command Buffers

Applications never execute kernels directly.

Instead they build immutable command buffers.

Begin

↓

Dispatch

↓

Copy

↓

Barrier

↓

Dispatch

↓

End

Once finalized, command buffers cannot change.

This enables:

replay
caching
serialization
debugging
deterministic execution
Kernel Model

A Kernel is immutable executable compute logic.

Kernel

+

Parameters

+

Resources

↓

Dispatch

Kernels contain no runtime state.

State belongs to resources.

Resource Model

Resources are independent objects.

Examples include

Buffers

Images

Textures

Acceleration Structures

Constants

Temporary Memory

Resources are reference counted.

Lifetime is explicit.

Memory Ownership

Every allocation belongs to exactly one Device.

Device

↓

Allocator

↓

Memory

↓

Buffer

Cross-device copies require explicit transfer commands.

Implicit migration is prohibited.

Scheduler

The Scheduler owns execution ordering.

Responsibilities include

Queue balancing

Dependency resolution

Resource hazards

Execution priority

Synchronization

Backend dispatch

Applications never interact with backend threads directly.

Synchronization

Synchronization primitives include

Fence

Semaphore

Barrier

Event

Synchronization is explicit.

Hidden synchronization is forbidden.

Backend Interface

Every backend implements the same interface.

initialize()

shutdown()

create_device()

compile_kernel()

submit()

wait()

copy()

destroy()

No backend may expose additional public API.

Backend-specific extensions belong behind capability queries.

Capability System

Devices expose supported capabilities.

Example

supports_fp16

supports_fp64

supports_tensor

supports_raytrace

supports_shared_memory

supports_atomic64

Applications query capabilities.

They never detect backend names.

Software Backend

A complete CPU backend is mandatory.

Reasons include

CI testing

Debugging

Deterministic execution

Platforms without GPUs

Education

Reference implementation

The CPU backend is considered a first-class implementation rather than a fallback.

Error Handling

Recoverable errors return Result types.

Fatal backend failures invalidate the Device.

Undefined behavior is prohibited.

Debug Layer

Every backend may expose optional debugging.

The debug layer can

Validate command buffers

Check resource hazards

Detect synchronization errors

Measure timing

Record execution traces

The debug layer must never alter execution semantics.

Future Compatibility

This architecture intentionally supports future execution targets including

Distributed compute

Cloud execution

Virtual devices

Software simulation

FPGA implementations

Custom accelerators

AI inference hardware

without modifying application code.

Non-Goals

This RFC does not define

Kernel language

Shader compiler

Memory allocator implementation

Scheduling algorithms

Backend implementations

Performance guarantees

Those are specified in future RFCs.

Rationale

The execution model is the stable foundation upon which the remainder of JGPU is built.

Applications interact exclusively with abstract compute concepts.

Backends translate those concepts into vendor-specific implementations.

This inversion allows JGPU to evolve independently of graphics APIs and hardware vendors while maintaining a stable programming interface.

Looking Ahead

If RFC001 defined what JGPU is, RFC002 defines how JGPU thinks. The natural progression after this is:

RFC-003 — Memory & Resource Model (allocators, buffers, views, mapping, residency)
RFC-004 — Kernel ABI & Dispatch Semantics
RFC-005 — Backend Plugin Interface
RFC-006 — JGPU Intermediate Representation (JIR)
RFC-007 — Software Reference Backend
RFC-008 — Virtual Device & Distributed Execution

That sequence would establish the architectural contract before any implementation details, giving the project a solid foundation to build against.
